.\" Manual page for rdp-session(8).  mdoc format.
.Dd May 29, 2026
.Dt RDP-SESSION 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rdp-session
.Nd per-user session helper for the Remote Desktop Protocol server
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl DW
.Op Fl w Ar width
.Op Fl H Ar height
.Op Fl k Ar lcid
.Op Fl z Ar tz
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the unprivileged per-user component of the Remote Desktop
Protocol server.  It is started by
.Xr rdp-sessionmgr 8
after authentication, with one end of a backend RPC socket pre-opened
on file descriptor 3.  By default it launches
.Xr Xvfb 1 ,
captures damaged regions with the XDamage extension, injects input
via the XTEST extension, and bridges the X11 CLIPBOARD selection
to the
.Sy CLIPRDR
RDP channel for plain text, HTML (the X
.Sy text/html
target is mapped to the registered Windows
.Sy HTML Format ) ,
and images (the X
.Sy image/bmp
target is mapped to the Windows
.Sy CF_DIB
and
.Sy CF_DIBV5
formats by a zero-dependency BMP conversion).
Files are copied in both directions over the X
.Sy text/uri-list
target and the Windows
.Sy FileGroupDescriptorW
format with on-demand
.Sy FILECONTENTS
transfers: a file copied in the session can be pasted in the client, and
a file copied in the client is downloaded into a per-session temporary
directory and exposed to the local application as a
.Sy file://
URI.
The client-supplied file names are untrusted, so each is sanitized
(back-slashes folded, and any empty,
.Sy \&. ,
.Sy \&.. ,
or absolute component rejected) before use, so a paste cannot escape
that temporary directory.
Large transfers are reassembled and read or written incrementally,
bounded at 4 MiB.
Scancode key events map directly to X keycodes; the Xvfb keyboard
layout is set from the client's reported layout (LCID, passed by
.Xr rdp-sessionmgr 8
via
.Fl k )
using
.Xr setxkbmap 1 ,
so those keycodes produce the client's characters (US is used for an
unknown layout).
Unicode key events are injected by temporarily remapping a spare
keycode to the target keysym and faking a press and release.
The client's lock-key state
.Pq Caps , Num , and Scroll Lock
is applied to the session keyboard with the XKB lock modifiers, so a
client that connects or regains focus with a lock engaged stays in
sync with the session.
.Pp
Touch and pen input from the client
.Pq MS-RDPEI, forwarded by Xr rdpd 8 as RDP_BE_INPUT_TOUCH
is injected into the session.
On the Wayland backend each contact drives a real
.Sy wl_touch
device, so multi-finger gestures and pen input reach the session
applications.
On Xvfb there is no virtual multitouch device, so the primary contact is
emulated as a single left pointer via the XTEST extension.
.Pp
When the client requested RemoteApp mode
.Pq MS-RDPERP , Sy RAIL , see Xr rdpd 8 ,
.Xr rdpd 8
tells the session to present its windows as seamless RemoteApp windows
rather than a flat desktop.
On the Wayland backend each
.Sy xdg_shell
toplevel keeps its natural size and a cascade position, and its
geometry is forwarded to
.Xr rdpd 8
so the client paints it as its own borderless window; opening, resizing,
and closing a window are reported as RAIL window create, update, and
delete events.
On the Xvfb backend the whole session desktop is reported as a single
RemoteApp window.
.Pp
The client's time zone, passed by
.Xr rdp-sessionmgr 8
via
.Fl z
as a POSIX
.Ev TZ
string, is exported into the session environment before the user shell
is started, so the session clock and applications show the client's
local time.  The string is synthesized by
.Xr rdpd 8
from the client's
.Sy TS_TIME_ZONE_INFORMATION
and is empty for a client that sends none, in which case the session
keeps the server's zone.
.Pp
Captured frames are sent as raw pixels over the backend socket.
The
.Xr rdpd 8
proxy encodes them to H.264 with
.Sy libx264
and wraps the bitstream in RDPGFX AVC420 PDUs when the client
negotiated the graphics pipeline; otherwise it sends fast-path
bitmap updates.
.Pp
The session mouse cursor is tracked with the X
.Sy XFixes
extension and forwarded over the backend socket whenever it changes.
The
.Xr rdpd 8
proxy relays it to the client as a fast-path New Pointer update so the
client shows the real session cursor instead of a static arrow.
A cursor identical to one already sent is relayed as a cached-pointer
reference into the client's pointer cache rather than re-sending the image.
.Pp
Audio from applications in the session is captured and forwarded to
the RDP client via the backend protocol.
The audio capture backend is selected at compile time:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
PulseAudio on Linux (auto-creates a per-session null sink).
.It
.Xr sndio 7
on
.Ox .
.It
CoreAudio on macOS.
.El
.Pp
In the other direction, if the client redirects its microphone over the
MS-RDPEAI
.Sy AUDIO_INPUT
channel, the captured PCM is presented inside the session as a PulseAudio
capture source named
.Sy rdp_microphone
.Pq a Sy module-pipe-source No fed over a per-session FIFO ,
so applications can select the client's microphone as their input.
This is best effort: with no PulseAudio or no redirected microphone the
session simply runs without the source.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl D
Use the native Xorg DDX driver
.Pq rdpserverdev_drv.so
instead of Xvfb.
The DDX driver renders directly to a POSIX shared memory framebuffer
and reports damage via a control socket, sending only changed regions
to
.Xr rdpd 8 .
.It Fl W
Use the Wayland compositor instead of X11.
Requires
.Sy libwlroots
0.18 or later.
Wayland-native clients connect to the embedded compositor directly.
In RemoteApp mode this backend reports each toplevel as its own seamless
window, where Xvfb reports the whole desktop as one window.
.It Fl w Ar width
Desktop width in pixels.
The default is 1024.
.It Fl H Ar height
Desktop height in pixels.
The default is 768.
.It Fl k Ar lcid
Client keyboard layout identifier (Windows LCID, decimal) used to set
the Xvfb keyboard layout.
0 or an unknown value selects the US layout.
.It Fl z Ar tz
Client time zone as a POSIX
.Ev TZ
string.
It is exported into the session environment so the session clock matches
the client.
An empty value keeps the server's zone.
.El
.Sh DRIVE REDIRECTION
On Linux the drives the client redirects over the RDPDR channel are
presented inside the session as a real file system mounted at
.Pa ~/RemoteDrive .
.Xr rdp-sessionmgr 8
opens
.Pa /dev/fuse
and mounts it on that directory before dropping privileges, then passes
the FUSE device on file descriptor 4.
.Nm
speaks the raw FUSE kernel protocol on that descriptor (no
.Sy libfuse
dependency) and translates browse, read, write, and namespace requests
.Pq lookup, getattr, setattr, opendir, readdir, open, read, write, create, mknod, mkdir, unlink, rmdir, and rename
into RDPDR file operations carried to the
.Xr rdpd 8
worker over the backend socket.
File metadata
.Pq size and timestamps
is fetched from the client with QueryInformation IRPs, so
.Xr stat 2
reports the true size and modification time.
Writing, truncating
.Pq including save-with-truncate ,
and timestamp updates are forwarded to the client; the mount is
read-write.
Creating a file or directory opens it with disposition FILE_CREATE;
deleting opens with DELETE and FILE_DELETE_ON_CLOSE and then sets
FileDispositionInformation; renaming sets FileRenameInformation.
A rename is only honoured when the source and destination are on the
same redirected drive; a cross-drive rename returns
.Er EXDEV
so the kernel falls back to a copy plus delete.
This Linux backend has been validated against a live mount with stat,
ls, cat, write/read-back, mkdir, and unlink, in addition to the
protocol-level unit test.
.Pp
On OpenBSD the same file system is presented through the kernel's own
.Pa fusebuf
protocol on
.Pa /dev/fuse
(messages correlated by a uuid, native
.Vt struct stat
attributes and native directory records) over the same
protocol-agnostic core, so the identical read and write operations are
supported.
This OpenBSD backend is experimental but has been validated against a
live mount with stat, ls, cat, and write/read-back over a real
.Pa fusefs ,
in addition to the protocol-level unit test.
If
.Pa /dev/fuse
is unavailable, the kernel is too old, or the host is neither Linux nor
OpenBSD, the session simply runs without the mount and drive operations
remain reachable only through the backend protocol.
The mount is removed when the session process exits.
.Pp
Each printer the client redirects over the RDPDR channel is exposed
inside the session as a CUPS print queue.
When the worker announces a printer,
.Nm
sanitizes its name into a CUPS queue name (an
.Sy rdp-
prefix, with characters outside
.Sy [A-Za-z0-9_-]
mapped to
.Sq _ )
and runs
.Xr lpadmin 8
to create a RAW queue whose device URI points at a per-session
.Ux Ns -domain
socket served by the session.
The companion CUPS backend
.Sy rdp-cups-backend ,
installed as
.Pa /usr/lib/cups/backend/rdp ,
forwards each job's spool to that socket;
.Nm
relays it to the worker (bounded at 4 MiB), which drives the MS-RDPEPC
print sequence to the client.
Printer redirection is best-effort: if
.Xr lpadmin 8
is absent or a queue cannot be created (for example the session user is
not a CUPS administrator), it is logged and skipped and the session is
unaffected.
The created queues are removed and the socket unlinked when the session
exits.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/RemoteDrive
Mount point for redirected client drives (Linux, read-write; OpenBSD
read-write but experimental).
.It Pa /usr/lib/cups/backend/rdp
The
.Sy rdp-cups-backend
helper, installed by hand (root owned, mode 0755) so the system cupsd can
forward redirected print jobs.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rdpd 8 ,
.Xr rdp-sessionmgr 8 ,
.Xr lpadmin 8
